            <Text profile="CFANoteText" id="hayNote" text="The Field Cure box shows the farmland you are on (a panel on the right when driving). Grass and sun icon = curing, green bale = ready to bale (20 % or less), blue drop = rained on. Overnight dew can lift hay back over 20 % - wait for it to dry off. Under 0.05 acre is not listed. Late fall (October-November in most of the U.S.): shorter days, weaker sun and heavy night dew slow drying a lot. Hay can take 1-2 weeks, or may not reach 20 %. Tedding spreads the grass thinner so it dries faster. Tedding drier grass loses more - alfalfa loses about 5 times as much (leaves shatter). Winter: a hard winter can thin alfalfa and grass stands, and the Fields tab shows each stand. Most growers replant alfalfa after the seeding year plus 2-3 years of cutting (University of Minnesota)."/>
